[38;5;231m⏺[39m The Keeper of Halden Point

  For thirty-one years, Edwin Marsh climbed the one hundred and twelve iron steps of Halden Point every evening at dusk, wound the clockwork that turned the great lens, and lit the lamp. His father
  had done the same, and his grandfather before him. The light had never once gone out on a night when a Marsh was keeping it.

  The island was small, a knuckle of granite a mile off the coast, and in winter the sea came over the rocks so hard that Edwin could feel it through the tower walls. He liked that. It reminded him
  the world was bigger than the cottage, bigger than the ledger where he logged ship passages, bigger than the letters that came every few months from his daughter in Lisbon, who wrote that he should
  come ashore and live properly.

  On the night of the November storm, the wind found every gap in the shutters. Edwin climbed the steps with a lantern in one hand and a rope tied to his belt, because the stairwell swayed when the
  gusts hit. At the top, the glass hummed. The lens was intact. The lamp caught on the first try.

  Then he saw the boat.

  It was a small fishing smack, low in the water, its sail torn to ribbons. Edwin could make out two figures clinging to the mast. He had no radio, only the old brass speaking tube that went down to
  the cottage, where his wife had died four winters ago. He picked up the signal horn and pulled it three times, then again, long and steady, as if the sound might carry a message the wind would not.

  He could not go down to the rocks. He could not row out. He could only keep the light burning and aim its beam at the one channel between the reef teeth, the path that would take a boat to safety.

  For two hours he stood at the lens, turning the lamp's hood by hand whenever the beam drifted, his palms raw from the brass. The sea roared. Once, a wave broke so high it struck the lower windows,
  and he thought of letting go of the rail, of simply sitting down on the cold floor. He did not.

  Near four in the morning, a shout rose from the dark, thin and faint. Then another. Then, through the gap in the reef, a dim lantern appeared, bobbing, moving in the channel the light had shown it.

  Edwin sank onto the stool beside the lens and laughed until he was crying, a sound the empty tower swallowed whole.

  At dawn he climbed down to meet the two fishermen on the landing stage. They were soaked and shaking, and one of them, a boy no older than his daughter had been when she left, asked him how he had
  known where the rocks were.

  "I didn't," Edwin said. "The light did. I only kept it lit."

  He wrote the night in the ledger that afternoon, as he wrote every night. [3mStorm. Wind southwest, very heavy. Light maintained. Two lives brought to shore.[0m Then, after a moment, he added one more
  line, and underlined it.

  [3mWorth the climb.[0m

